  frq: {
    prompt: 'Compare a lipoma with an invasive ductal adenocarcinoma of the breast. Explain the differences in cell appearance, growth and spread, explain what “grade 3, T2 N1 M0” would mean for the carcinoma, and identify two risk factors and one screening test for breast cancer.',
    rubric: [['Contrasts benign lipoma (encapsulated, well differentiated, no spread) with malignant adenocarcinoma (invasive, poorly differentiated, metastasizes)', ['benign', 'malignant', 'encapsul', 'invade', 'metasta']], ['Explains grade 3 as poorly differentiated, aggressive-appearing cells', ['grade', 'poorly differentiated', 'aggressive', 'abnormal']], ['Interprets T2 N1 M0 correctly', ['tumor', 'lymph node', 'no distant', 'metastasis', 'm0']], ['Names valid risk factors and a screening test', ['brca', 'age', 'family', 'estrogen', 'alcohol', 'obesity', 'mammogra']]],
    model: 'A lipoma is a benign tumor of fat: its cells are well differentiated and look like normal fat cells, it grows slowly, is usually encapsulated, soft and movable, and it does not invade or metastasize. An invasive ductal adenocarcinoma is a malignant tumor of glandular epithelium: its cells are less differentiated with abnormal nuclei, it grows faster, invades surrounding tissue and can spread through lymphatics and blood. Grade 3 means the cells are poorly differentiated and look very abnormal, suggesting more aggressive behavior. T2 N1 M0 means a moderate-size primary tumor (T2), spread to nearby lymph nodes such as the axillary nodes (N1), and no distant metastasis (M0). Risk factors include female sex and older age, inherited BRCA1 or BRCA2 mutations, family history, prolonged estrogen exposure, alcohol and obesity after menopause. Mammography is the main screening test.',
  },
